Safety awareness in cooperation with Tsuruoka police station and related organizations|The Yamagata Shimbun
In the bicycle class held as the start event of the operation, a collision experiment between a car and a bicycle was conducted = Shoko Gakuen Driving School in Tsuruoka City
The Tsuruoka Police Station (Chief Superintendent Kagezo Sato) has recently launched the "Operation bye-by-accident" in order to eliminate accidents involving bicycles and motorcycles, which have a high rate of occurrences occurring in pipes, in recent years.
In addition to working with traffic safety organizations in the prefecture to raise awareness of accident prevention, they will work to strengthen the crackdown on bicycle traffic rule violations.
There have been three traffic fatalities this year, and people who used motorcycles and motorcycles have died.
Although the number of accidents has been on a decreasing trend, the proportion of accidents involving bicycles is about 11.7 percent as of June 30, and the number of accidents related to motorcycles is about 5.8%, both of which are higher than the prefectural average.
In particular, bicycle accidents in the past five years have been more frequent than the prefectural-average, so we planned a strategy to overcome this situation.
"bike" and "bicycle" were combined with the meaning of accident eradication.
In traffic safety classrooms, related organizations are required to provide driving guidance to the elderly and the use of luminescent reflectors.
At the same police station, yellow cards (guidance warning ballots) will be issued at the time of policing.
On the 21st, the commencement ceremony of the operation was held at Shoko Gakuen Driving School in Tsuruoka City.
"I would like to ask for your cooperation in order to reduce the number of tragic accidents."
The person in charge of the office indicated the policy and the contents of the activities of each organization.
A bicycle class will be held as the start event of the operation.
A policeman in a car demonstrated how the impact on the bicycle is different, such as when a police officer hits a bicycle by applying the brake to hit a bicycle, or when it collides at about 55km per hour without stepping on the brake.
Mr. Koichi Kaneuchi, chairman of the Tsuruoka Regional Traffic Safety Activity Promotion Council, said, "I felt the horror of the traffic accident once again. I want to call attention not only to the elderly but also to young people."
