The Macau casino superintendent observes the implementation of epidemic prevention measures at the casino facility ... It is mandatory to present a new coronavirus negative certificate at the time of entry (Macau newspaper) - Yahoo! News
About 40 casino facilities in Macau have been closed for 15 days from February 5 to 19 due to the COVID-19 pandemic, and have resumed operations after taking strict anti-epidemic measures since the 20th of the same month.
According to the Macau Casino Regulatory Authority (DICJ), when reopening, the operating company is required to protect the health of employees and guests as much as the best possible protection of the health of the employees and guests, to ensure the distance between the gaming (casino) tables, to vacate neighboring seats in table games (e.g. three to four people can be seated at one table at the same time in the baccarat table), and the slot machine is also set to be operated every other month, and the risk of cross-infection has been mitigated by reducing the risk of cross-infection by reducing the risk of cross-infection.
Disinfection of gaming items such as chips has also been strengthened.
In addition, guests are required to wear a mask at the time of entry, measure their temperature, and present an effective health code (the most recent residence history, whether they have a history of contact with the new coronavirus patient, whether there are symptoms such as fever and cough, and those that are generated by entering the contact information).
From midnight on July 15th, the presentation of the new coronavirus nucleic acid test negative certificate (limited to those within the validity period issued by the approved facility in Macau or Guangdong Province) was also added to the condition.
The background to the mandatory presentation of a new negative certificate is the relaxation of border measures between Macau and Guangdong Province from 6am on the 15th (the elimination of quarantine for fourteen days, there are various terms and conditions), and it will be a preventative measure associated with this.
In response to a change in the entry conditions from the 15th of the same month, DICJ announced on July 18th that it had observed the implementation status of epidemic prevention measures at the casino facilities, and held a meeting with representatives of the security departments of the six casino operating companies to thoroughly manage the admissions of guests, and reiterated its request to protect the health of its employees and guests to protect the health of its employees and guests.
The DICJ will continue to carry out strict patrol patrols of casino facilities in the future, and will have regular meetings with the operating companies in accordance with the situation and the evaluation, and indicated the policy of adjusting the epidemic prevention measures at casinos as appropriate in accordance with the guidelines of the government health department.
The visit to the casino facilities was also accompanied by Mr. Hayashi Matsu, director of the Center for Disease Supervising and Prevention of the Sanitation Bureau of the Ministry of Sanitation, who provided guidance on disinfection of gaming (casino) chips and how to take a safe distance between the seats of electronic casino equipment.
In addition, due to a drastic decrease in the number of inbound tourists due to anti-epidemic prevention measures including entry restrictions, casino sales in the January-June period of this year decreased by 77.4% from the same period of the previous fiscal year to 337.2 billion Macau Pataca (Japanese yen equivalent: about 451.9 billion yen).
