Masujiro Omura's Statue of Masujiro Omura as an Educator [Yamaguchi]|Ube Nippo This Town Media Web
Masujiro Omura (1825-69), who is one of the ten outstanding figures of the Meiji Restoration, was seen on the 23rd at Uchinzenji, Yamaguchi City, his birthplace.
An unveiling ceremony was held in the forecourt of the Mushisenji Regional Exchange Center, which was set up, and local and government officials celebrated its completion.
Masujiro was a military scholar at the end of the Edo period and the Meiji Restoration, a Western scholar, and a doctor.
After serving as a teacher at "Bansoshirabesho," a research and educational institution for Western studies in the bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun), he set up a private cram school "Hatari-do" in Edo to teach Western studies, military studies, and medicine.
Even after returning to the Choshu Domain, he gave a lecture on militarism at Meirin YAMAGUCHI.
The bronze statue focused on Masujiro as an Educator.
He stood beside the terrestrial, held a book in his hand, and took the form of giving a lecture on Western studies.
The pedestal was about three metres (55m), and it is said to have been created from the perspective of a disciple who received a lecture by Masujiro.
It was designed by 27-year-old local designer Marie Takatane, a descendant of Kotoko, Masujiro’s wife.
Masujiro OMURA's 150th-year business executive committee (Chairman Satoshi Okamoto), whose parent body is Masujiro Omura's parent organization, solicited donations and built it.
A collection of about 1280 million yen worth of Jozai was collected from 1104 people across the country.
About twelve million yen in project expenses, such as the installation of mana-ita.
The remaining donations will be used for the maintenance and maintenance of the statue.
Chairman Okamoto said, "Masufujiro is proud of Furusato who has made a great contribution as an educator with high aspirations and has made a great contribution as an educator. It is a bronze statue that is the hope of the children of the region who will bear the future."
