It turns out that the white paint derived from the frying pan of Teflon-processed, the thermal insulation enhances the cooling effect!
What a great discovery!
This year, the rainy season was conspicuous due to an unusual amount of rainfall, but if it turns out, the extremely hot summer will be in full swing.
It's hot and hot, the scorching days when even the air-conditioning doesn't even work ... just imagining it already, it's almost likely that you'll groan, don't you?
Global warming is a major global problem, and human beings have narrowed down their intelligence on how to overcome the hot midsummer a little more comfortably.
The only way to do this is to wear light white clothing.
You don’t have to wear black clothes that absorb heat in the summer, do you?
The same principle is applied to the architecture of the house, and it seems that there are an increasing number of cases where white paint is used.
The latest field of research has also been the most recent field of research that cannot be turned into a paint with characteristics that reflect more light and does not absorbs heat, even if it is white.
The Henry Samueli School of Engineering and Applied Science at the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA), in collaboration with researchers from Columbia University’s Department of Applied Physics and Applied Numbers, announced that they have found a method of preparing white paint that can most efficiently repel solar heat and increase the cooling effect inside the building.
It is said that early commercialization will be targeted.
So far, the white paint, which has the highest reflectivity of the sun's rays, has used titanium oxide, which has a reflectance rate of 85%.
White paint, which already uses titanium dioxide, is applied to the roofs of buildings in high-temperature environments and is used to block solar heat and improve cooling efficiency.
However, what was newly discovered was a white paint derived from the fluoropolymers of polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE), which is used in that Teflon-treated frying pan.
It is a white paint that applies this Teflon effect, and it is said to be capable of a significant heat cut by reflecting the sun's rays up to about 98%.
Even in the hot summer, thanks to white paint using Tefcuron unexpectedly, it is easy to spend indoors without being exposed to extreme heat.
Such a time may be near, isn’t it?
I feel that the roof of a Japanese house has a lot of dark colors, but from now on, white will become the mainstream?
