The mobile number transfer, a severe eye for over-retention.
What will happen to fees and points?
(NEW SWITCH) - Yahoo! News
The Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications (MNP) is in full-scale consideration for the review of the MNP system (MNP), in which conventional telephone numbers can be used as it is when changing mobile communication companies.
At a meeting of experts on the 21st, he indicated a policy of amending the MNP's guidelines, which were enacted in FY2004.
There are a wide-ranging range of specific issues, such as the nature of MNP fees and the pros and cons of holding back for those who wish to relocate.
While telecommunications companies are also expected to attract a backlash, attention will be focused on whether it will lead to the promotion of healthy market competition.
All-you-can-use mobile phone transfer is not far The darkness of telecommunications companies MNP was introduced, and the user charge was 2000yen, but it is now 3000yen.
The MNP fee revenue of the three major mobile telecommunications companies is estimated to be 159billion yen a year.
On the 21st, the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications (MIC) listed the current situation of MNP in the working group (WG on the verification of competition rules), a meeting of experts to discuss the competitive environment in the telecommunications market.
The time required for consumers to issue an MNP reservation number when moving out to another telecommunications company was also cited.
Major telecommunications companies accept it on the web, but due to the convenience of the operation of the core system, the acceptance time is mainly from 9th to 20th.
In the WG materials, it was clearly stated that the direction of future consideration is to respond throughout the day in principle.
For those who wish to move out with MNP, mobile communication companies are also seen as a problem by granting points.
Excessive restraint could hinder fair competition.
Shunichi Kita, a partner at Nomura Research Institute, which is a member of the WG, recommends that "it is important to thoroughly implement this process first."
However, in a series of points of contention, MNP commissions have a structural problem that “the higher it is, the more difficult it is for customers to run out, so it will be difficult to motivate them to actively lower it” (the person in charge of the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications).
In the event of a company that takes the lead in lowering MNP fees, there will be an increase in customer outflows from the company, and there are also concerns that the company's efforts will not be rewarded.
The Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications has also pointed out that many countries overseas do not ask for user-burden fees for MNPs, and there is a possibility that this may be the case in Japan.
The Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications would like to compile the WG's achievements as a report around September-October, and then start the procedure for amending the MNP guidelines.
It is expected that the period when the amendments will be carried out after twenty-one years.
Mobile telecommunications companies are likely to be scratching their minds, including sales and customer support.
