Masuda-jinja Shrine cancels the annual grand festival Martyr's sergeant-at-arms for efforts to prevent cholera The influence of Takagushi, Hizen-cho, Karatsu-shi, Karatsu-shi: Coronavirus: Shinto rituals only held today|News from the town|Saga Shimbun News|Saga Shimbun LiVE
Masuda-jinja Shrine, which enshrines Officer Masuda who was martyred to prevent cholera = Tano, Hizen-cho, Karatsu City
Officer Masuda's float on a white horse that paraded through the district = Karatsu City Hizen Machino (provided)
Masuda-jinja Shrine, located in the Takagushi area of Tano, Hizen-cho, Karatsu City, celebrates Officer Keitaro Masuda (1869-95), a martyr who made efforts to deal with cholera, an infectious disease that spread to the region 125 years ago.
Every year at the end of July, an annual big festival has been held grandly, but this year it was canceled due to the influence of the new coronavirus.
On the 26th, the self-governing presidents perform only Shinto rituals.
A self-governing association has held an annual festival, and a parade of sergeant Masuda's floats on a white horse and the launch of fireworks.
Nearly two hundred people, including bereaved families, police officials, and local residents living in Kumamoto Prefecture, attend every year.
It is said that the number of inquiries for interviews from the media increased this year due to the coronavirus pandemic, and Ryotoku Takeda, 72, the chairman of the self-governing government, said, "It's crowded every year at an annual festival that we've been connected to the local area, and we're very sorry for the cancellation."
The prefectural police also canceled a memorial service visited by police school students this year.
On the 21st, Yumiko Sugiuchi, the head of the headquarters, and Hiroto Fukushima, the chief of Karatsu Police Station, visited Komatsujima, which is located off the coast of Takagushi-minato, which was attached to Dabi, and offered the flowers to the monument.
"The spirit of bravery and dedication is the starting point of the policing, and I would like to pass it on to what it should be," said Mr. Sugiuchi.
Officer Masuda, who was born in Kumamoto Prefecture, moved to Takagushi in July 1895.
In order to take preventive measures such as disinfecting the patient's house with insomnia, he himself was infected with cholera.
He died at the age of twenty-five on just the 4th day after he arrived.
